00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.710
there are tens of thousands
of communities around whatever

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.789
passions or interests
you might have,

00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:10.580
where people converse
about the things that

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.380
are most important to them.

00:01:15.579 --> 00:01:17.120
For those of you
who know what Reddit

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.007
is, you may not know
some of these statistics

00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:20.090
I'm about to throw at you.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:23.510
Reddit is, I would say,
kind of a big deal--

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:26.030
fourth largest site
in the United States,

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.210
according to Alexa, sixth
largest in the world,

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.580
350 million monthly
active users,

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:35.660
10.7 million posts per month,
2.8 million comments per day,

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.070
58 million votes per day.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.560
And then getting back to
the theme of communities,

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.830
more than one million
communities on the site.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:47.070
And when we think about
communities this year,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.510
particularly 2018,
Reddit is really

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.590
trying to transform itself
in the following way.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.530
We want to be able
to connect people

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.990
to the best, most interesting
communities for them.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:58.490
For those of you
in the audience who

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.170
have been on Reddit
for a long time,

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.530
one question I often ask
our users is how long did it

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.270
take for you to find
your first community?

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.940
For me, that was
about four years.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.009
We're trying to get that
number down to maybe

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.410
a few minutes, would be great.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.850
So Reddit is this vast
network of communities-- news,

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:18.050
politics, gaming, sports, all
sorts of different content

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:19.580
we've tried to visualize here.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.680
And the question we've
been wrestling with

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:25.460
is how do we connect people
with the right communities?

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.770
Now, later on, you're
going to hear from Sergei,

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:29.180
and he's going to
talk about some

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.850
really, really advanced
techniques and recommendations.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.120
But I thought as a cue
into that discussion,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.310
perhaps we could take
a trip down memory lane

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.980
and talk about the history
of Reddit's data platform,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.000
if you will indulge me.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.330
So I'll start in
2006, the dark ages,

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.270
trying to solve this problem of
recommending content to users.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.210
Our first approach was
collaborative filtering.

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.670
The way Reddit works, for
those of you have seen it,

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.740
we have a system of up
votes and down votes.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.900
So one might naturally
think that if you

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.750
have the same up voting, down
voting pattern as someone

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:07.220
else on the site, perhaps you
would recommend similar things

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.270
to each other.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:11.030
And this was state
of the art for 2006.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:14.300
We represented users
as a matrix with posts

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.830
in what they voted on.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:18.500
We used cosine
similarity distance,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.870
recentered by subtracting
the global mean,

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.650
and then applied SVD
to get the results.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.910
Now, great for 2006.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.356
It turns out it
just doesn't work.

00:03:29.356 --> 00:03:31.730
We had a couple problems that
we were trying to overcome,

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.530
data sparseness.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.010
You may not realize it
now, but back in 2006,

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.020
Reddit didn't even
have communities.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.570
It was just one
front page listing,

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.050
one big pile of content.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.980
So not a lot of interesting
dimensions to look at.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.670
The median number of votes
per user was pretty much one.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:53.070
So very few people
actually voting.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:56.270
And this meant that if you have
the combination of everything

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.940
only showing up on one page
with very few people voting,

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.784
you really get
this mean response

00:04:00.784 --> 00:04:02.450
behavior, where you're
just recommending

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.560
the same stuff to everybody.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.340
And we had no feedback loop
on that except for-- this is

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.510
an example we've taken
from nine years ago--

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.880
why does the recommended
feature suck so bad?

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.270
I routinely down vote every
American politics story.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:17.790
My first two recommended pages
are nothing but politics.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:18.950
OK.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.870
But there was a
use case for this,

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.930
if you can imagine,
vote cheating.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.590
So what is vote cheating?

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:28.220
Let me explain why
you would do this.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.620
Reddit, if you can get your
content to the front page,

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:31.870
it's actually pretty valuable.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.790
Several million people
are going to see that.

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:35.780
So to cheat Reddit
back in the day,

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:38.390
create a bunch of bot
accounts, have them all up vote

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:41.540
your content, and profit.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:45.800
And it turns out that these
bots, these cheating accounts,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.880
also have very, very
similar voting patterns.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:52.100
You can actually make a
visualization of that.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:55.370
This is clusters of people
who have similar voting

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:56.780
behavior on the site.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.180
And you can see
that in the center,

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:02.431
there is the standard behavior,
a lot of mean response voting

00:05:02.431 --> 00:05:02.930
behavior.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:06.560
But you can see islands of these
unique users off to the side.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.300
And these are cheaters.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:10.220
So we did find a
way to use that.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.417
Now, from 2008 to
2014, for those of you

00:05:12.417 --> 00:05:14.000
who understand
Reddit's history, we'll

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.510
call this the
dramatic intermission.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:20.960
Nothing really happened on
Reddit from a data perspective,

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.900
but the site did
continue to grow.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:25.970
One great advantage
of working at Reddit

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:27.650
is the site is just addictive.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:30.350
We call it weaponized
procrastination.

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:32.810
And it will grow
in and of itself.

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:36.500
So between 2008, 2014,
it's growing by itself.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:42.650
But then the gears start to
come back into motion in 2014.

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:46.310
This is Reddit's first real
data and analytics pipeline.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:49.040
Everything previous to this was
essentially just a lot of data

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.680
from logs sitting in Postgres.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.470
Here we've got Kafka
dumping data in S3,

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.600
being processed by Hive.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.790
And as you can
imagine, for those

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:01.200
of you who have dealt with
Hive, this is kind of slow.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.020
It's not really operational.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:05.930
Really what this got used
for was every two months,

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:08.730
Reddit has a board meeting and
the deck needs to be created.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.490
So someone would go
kick the Hive jobs

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.230
and generate the board deck.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.360
But it's better than nothing.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:18.090
2015, we start to get just a
little bit more sophisticated.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:18.835
We added Midas.

00:06:18.835 --> 00:06:20.210
For those of you
who know, that's

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:23.090
an open source project
that does enrichment.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:27.000
We used it to enrich
geolocation, user agent data,

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.530
et cetera.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:29.755
Another year goes by.

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:32.130
We're starting to get a little
bit more interesting here.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.240
This is when we can finally
do relevance and feedback.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:38.960
We've got two services here,
the blue and yellow one.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.860
Minsky, for those of you
folks who are into classic AI,

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.950
Minsky's named
after Marvin Minsky.

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:46.990
And this is essentially
our first attempt

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:51.022
at allowing the home page
to be generated dynamically

00:06:51.022 --> 00:06:52.480
based on user
preference, and we'll

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.400
talk about that a
little bit more later.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.830
On the second box is Spamurai.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:01.480
And what that is is it uses
hand tuned rules in conjunction

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.730
with feedback from
the community in order

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:05.592
to try and dynamically
detect spam.

00:07:05.592 --> 00:07:07.300
And this is really
important because this

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.170
is the first feedback loop
that takes data analytics

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:14.360
and drives it back
into the product.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.720
2017, which
effectively brings us

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:19.820
very close to our
present-day system,

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:22.340
we began forking all
of our event data

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:24.290
into BigQuery after
considering a lot

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:25.840
of different alternatives.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.590
For those of you who
are considering this,

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:29.210
I highly recommend it.

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:31.980
Coincidentally, that's why
they invited me to talk here.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:33.360
[LAUGHS]

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.260
But BigQuery is actually
pretty freaking good.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:38.240
It's simpler to manage.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.850
It's got much faster
query performance.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.520
You can connect it to
a host of BI tools.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.310
We use Mode Looker or Power BI.

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:48.650
And on top of all that,
it's pretty freaking cheap,

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.460
which makes our COO very happy.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.330
So we can't say this
is a perfect solution.

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:54.870
I do have some
complaints about it,

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:57.830
but I don't really lose sleep
over thinking about our data

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:00.750
warehouse choices anymore.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:05.160
So with that pipeline in place,
let's reattempt this problem.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:06.950
How do we get community
recommendations

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:08.660
in this modern pipeline?

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:11.840
Well, we now have
the ability to get

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.200
core metrics, what our users
are doing, essentially,

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.920
in real time.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:20.780
This is a chart of us rolling
out the recent Reddit redesign.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.670
If you go to
www.reddit.com, you'll

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:24.030
get to see what that looks like.

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:26.870
This is us rolling out
that new system and data

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:29.190
streaming in real time.

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:33.380
This allows us to attempt
a few different approaches.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:39.072
Our first approach was
to use Jaccard distance.

00:08:39.072 --> 00:08:41.030
Sergei will get more
academic about this later,

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:43.549
but Jaccard distance
is simply a way

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:46.369
to calculate the similarity
between two sets.

00:08:46.369 --> 00:08:47.660
And this was our first attempt.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:50.930
If you think about each
subreddit and the people

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:53.420
within it, the people who have
subscribed to that subreddit

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:55.700
as being a set, you can
then use Jaccard distance

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.520
to compute the distance
between all of the subreddits

00:08:58.520 --> 00:08:59.340
on Reddit.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:01.986
The problems here are that
we have a lot of subreddits,

00:09:01.986 --> 00:09:03.110
about a million subreddits.

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:05.990
This algorithm scales
at O(n) squared.

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:08.374
So humans did the hard part
of essentially whitelisting

00:09:08.374 --> 00:09:10.540
the subreddits we thought
would be most interesting,

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.350
pruning out the ones
that were very, very

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:14.120
undersubscribed, for example.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:17.050
Second problem, we have a
ton of default subreddits.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.282
So in the olden days when
you would join Reddit,

00:09:19.282 --> 00:09:20.490
there was no onboarding flow.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:23.360
We would simply subscribe
you to about 20 subreddits.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:25.250
And this also polluted data.

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:29.360
But we used humans to
clean that up as well.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:32.630
Problem with that is it still
didn't work really well.

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:35.960
We still got a lot of
mean response behavior

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:38.030
even after doing that tuning.

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:41.780
But we had an insight
that was really important.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.810
Instead of trying to look at
user behavior, what people are

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.550
subscribing to and so forth,
why don't we just do something

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:48.508
a little bit different?

00:09:48.508 --> 00:09:50.870
The aha moment we had
is that every subreddit

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:55.130
and the comments and the text
that are part of the forum

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:57.340
really can be treated
as a bag of words.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:01.190
So can we get the subreddits
simply to describe themselves

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:02.450
and what they talk about?

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:06.080
So take all the
corpus of commentary

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.280
on a given subreddit, smash
that into a gigantic document,

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:12.230
run doc2vec on it.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.330
And now, we convert
each subreddit

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:16.160
into a 300-dimensional
vector that

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:19.760
represents the semantics of
what that subreddit is about.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:22.430
And that is the approach
we're using now.

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:24.620
And what this gives
us is we can now

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:26.730
recommend communities by topic.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:29.806
So if you onboard
into Reddit now,

00:10:29.806 --> 00:10:32.180
you'll be given an option to
choose different topics that

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:35.120
you're interested in-- health,
crypto, gaming, et cetera--

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.310
and we'll automatically
suggest what

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:40.190
we think are the most
semantically relevant

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.436
subreddits to that topic.

00:10:41.436 --> 00:10:43.310
So for health, you get
Fitness, Bodybuilding.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:48.470
For cryptocurrency, you get
BTC and EthTrader, et cetera.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:52.350
But maybe we can take
that a little further.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:55.760
What if you already have found
a community that you like?

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:58.640
Can we recommend similar
communities to the one

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:00.990
that you're already
interested in?

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:06.050
So we use a very similar
technique here using that data.

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:07.920
We can get similarity
to Overwatch.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:09.170
There's Competitive Overwatch.

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:11.730
There's Overwatch University,
which teaches you how to play.

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:14.330
There's Overwatch Memes,
which are essentially

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:17.012
little mini videos of people
playing Overwatch, et cetera.

00:11:17.012 --> 00:11:18.470
And then you can
do the same thing.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:19.920
If you guys are into
machine learning,

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:21.628
you can check out
Learn Machine Learning.

00:11:21.628 --> 00:11:23.150
Math, statistics,
data science, all

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:25.030
cover the topic of
machine learning

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:27.510
in some slightly different way.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:30.390
But maybe we can take
this even further.

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:35.120
What if we could dynamically
compute the main listings

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:35.870
feed for Reddit?

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:38.991
So when you go to reddit.com,
you'll see our popular listing.

00:11:38.991 --> 00:11:41.240
If you're signed in, you'll
see our home feed listing.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:43.760
What if we could make that
more personalized and use

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:46.000
signals such as what you
look at, what you click on,

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:46.920
et cetera?

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:48.770
Reddit's old home
feed algorithm,

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:50.480
if you guys were
wondering how it worked,

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:53.780
was the same ranking
algorithm for 12 years,

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:57.650
hand tuned by our most
brilliant engineer who's

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:59.253
sitting in the fourth row--

00:11:59.253 --> 00:12:02.330
[LAUGHS] --hand tuned
algorithm that we

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:03.620
think we could improve on.

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:09.290
So we attempted to use large
scale logistic regression using

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:12.020
time of day subreddits
you're subscribed to,

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:14.230
some interests that we
collect during onboarding,

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.210
the device you're on,
mobile or desktop,

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:18.640
and several other signals.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.220
10 terabytes of training
data, 50 million features,

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:23.290
five million parameters.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:24.850
And we currently
have five versions

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:27.320
of this model in production,
trying to choose the best one,

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:31.240
but we have some very, very
promising early results.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:35.160
Our early results are we get
a 12% time on site increase

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.260
with just our initial attempts.

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:40.185
And this is actually
extremely good.

00:12:40.185 --> 00:12:42.750
I'll explain to you
why on the next slide.

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:44.770
This is our Alexa rankings.

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:48.797
You can see Reddit, fourth
largest site on the web.

00:12:48.797 --> 00:12:51.130
And the important number here
is the daily time on site,

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:52.720
that column to the far right.

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:55.260
You can see Reddit
is already the most

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:57.300
addictive site on the internet.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.220
People who come to
Reddit stay there longer

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:00.930
than any other site
and we've managed

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:05.470
to increase that
addictiveness even further.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.470
Yeah, we're not letting you go.

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:10.522
Not until the GDP of the
world drops will we be done.

00:13:10.522 --> 00:13:11.406
[LAUGHS]

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:15.880
So that's great,
but what's next?

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:18.380
I want to introduce--

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.770
Sergei, you jumped at the gun.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:23.490
You've got to wait in the wings.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.380
I'm cueing you up, man.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:29.140
Now, all of these improvements
happened in about the last year

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:29.640
and a half.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:31.056
We're extremely
excited about what

00:13:31.056 --> 00:13:32.550
we've been able to accomplish.

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:35.670
Now, what I'm excited
to introduce to you

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:38.400
next is to show off some
work that my colleague

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:39.510
Sergei has done.

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:42.600
And what we view this
partnership with Google

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.220
as is what is possible?

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:46.200
What can happen
in the next year?

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:49.480
And he's given us a glimpse
of some amazing results.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:51.240
So please welcome
Sergei to the stage.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:52.936
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:52.936 --> 00:13:55.294
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:13:57.235 --> 00:13:59.860
SERGEI SOLOLENKO: Thanks, Nick,
for the wonderful introduction.

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:02.350
So what I wanted to
talk about to you today

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:05.680
was thinking of this concept
of using machine learning

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:08.560
using big data and data
processing, and trying

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:11.620
to come up with some
predictions of user behavior.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:16.000
Nick talked to you about how, by
building several recommendation

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.180
systems, they were able
to drive the time on site

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:21.280
to pretty high values.

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:25.450
So I asked myself
the same question.

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:29.440
What if we could predict
popularity of news articles

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:30.850
on Reddit?

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:33.250
Here's an example
of a news article.

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:36.760
It was written by one of my
colleagues, Martin Gardner.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:39.160
Some of you might have
heard his session today.

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:42.350
He continues writing about
TensorFlow and deep learning.

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:45.400
So this blog post is
pretty meta because it

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:48.640
is talking about machine
learning and TensorFlow.

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:50.290
And so the question
I ask myself,

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:53.170
if I look at this
blog post and if I

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:55.210
know a couple of
attributes about it,

00:14:55.210 --> 00:15:00.340
can I make predictions
about user behavior,

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:03.130
specifically on Reddit?

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:05.920
Now incidentally,
this blog post was

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:08.710
shared four times in four
different communities

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:10.870
by four different people.

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:14.740
So I chose it because it
provided me with some good data

00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:18.310
on user behavior, some
real user actions,

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:21.180
and it also was
published back in 2017.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:24.520
So now, I have about a
year worth of data points

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:29.370
that I can look back and train
my machine learning model on.

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:33.060
So what are these outcomes that
I want to be able to predict?

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:36.470
I want to deal with
predictors and outcomes.

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:40.070
And the outcome for
me is a user action

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:42.540
in my desired Reddit community.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:45.350
So the first prediction or
the first outcome would be,

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:50.480
what is the subreddit that
this blog post was posted in?

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:52.700
The other predictor--
the other outcome,

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:56.990
sorry, is the person who
decided to share this news

00:15:56.990 --> 00:15:59.300
article in the subreddit.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:03.704
Yet another outcome is
the popularity score.

00:16:03.704 --> 00:16:05.120
Those are the
numbers on the left.

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:09.140
And some of the postings achieve
pretty good scores, almost 100,

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:10.400
if not more.

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:13.480
Others only got maybe
a two or a five.

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:17.780
And lastly, one of
the possible outcomes

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:22.210
is the number of
people, users of Reddit,

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:25.970
who come and comment
on the posting

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:27.680
and have a
conversation about it.

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:29.910
OK, so now we
understood the outcomes.

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:33.030
Now, we understood how
users on the Reddit work.

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:34.670
Let's talk about predictors.

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:36.630
What are the possible
predictors for me

00:16:36.630 --> 00:16:41.210
to build my machine
learning model on?

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:46.940
One possible predictor is
the author of the blog post.

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:50.870
So the hunch is the person who
writes a news article or blog

00:16:50.870 --> 00:16:53.150
post probably has a
community of readers.

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:55.460
And these people
will come to Reddit,

00:16:55.460 --> 00:16:59.460
and perhaps there's an overlap
of the readers of the author,

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:01.910
as well as the users of Reddit.

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:03.880
And so these folks
will go and share it

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:06.599
in the community
of their choice.

00:17:06.599 --> 00:17:09.530
The other predictor
is the textual content

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:10.940
of the news article.

00:17:10.940 --> 00:17:12.589
That's the green
stuff in the middle.

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:15.290
That's the tags, the free
form tags, the topics

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.230
the news article is about.

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:22.250
Yet another possible predictor
would be the publication time

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:25.130
and perhaps the place,
the publication domain

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:28.109
where this news
article was posted on.

00:17:28.109 --> 00:17:30.650
So I've got four outcomes
that I'm interested in.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.680
I've got four
possible predictors.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:38.030
Before I jump into talking about
machine learning and TensorFlow

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:40.460
and deep learning, I want
to just quickly cover

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:42.240
the business domain.

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:45.560
It's always good to stop
and think about user actions

00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:47.540
and what the business is about.

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:50.420
I tend to think of Reddit
as a huge collection

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:52.490
of threaded conversations.

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:54.740
Someone writes a news article.

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:57.220
That person is typically
outside of Reddit.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:18:00.020
This article gets
posted to, I don't know,

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.960
the Google Big Data
blog or "New York Times"

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:07.670
or CNN.com or some other
publication domain.

00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:10.370
A user on Reddit
picks up this article,

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:13.400
reads it, becomes
interested in the content

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:17.990
and decides to share this
news article in a community

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:20.360
of his or her choice.

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:23.750
Then people on Reddit
come and begin commenting

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:28.940
and you have a conversation
on Reddit about this post.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:30.920
Across all of these
entities-- and now,

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:33.290
I'm switching back
into architecture stuff

00:18:33.290 --> 00:18:35.220
and talking about the
entity relationships.

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:37.820
So across all of these
entities, you always

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:40.900
have the person who
initiated this action.

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:43.100
And you also have some
differences between entities.

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:47.450
For example, the news article
will have textual content.

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:51.980
The blog-- sorry, the Reddit
post will have the subreddit.

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:56.290
And the comments will also
have some textual content.

00:18:56.290 --> 00:18:58.430
OK, so now we
understood the business.

00:18:58.430 --> 00:19:01.771
Let's go ahead and start
building our deep learning data

00:19:01.771 --> 00:19:02.270
set.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:04.280
And so this is our
first challenge.

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:07.780
And I'm going to talk
about how we solve this.

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:09.280
Ultimately, what
we are trying to do

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:11.830
is to bring two streams of data.

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:16.630
The first stream are
Reddit posts and comments.

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:20.110
The other stream of data are
the news posts or the news

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:21.450
articles.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:23.950
And so we were lucky that
both of these data streams

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:28.780
are actually available as
public data sets in BigQuery.

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:31.660
The Reddit posts
and comments are

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:34.570
available in a
BigQuery dataset that

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:37.020
is maintained by one of our
developer advocates, Felipe

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:37.840
Hoffa.

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.336
So hat tip to him,
thank you, Felipe.

00:19:40.336 --> 00:19:43.300
And it draws its data
from a collection

00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:48.220
of exports from Reddit that are
operated by Jason Baumgartner.

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:51.610
Thank you, Jason, for
keeping this work up.

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:53.140
Amazing job.

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:56.290
On the new site, we also
have an amazing data set.

00:19:56.290 --> 00:20:01.450
And I also want to reach
out to [INAUDIBLE] --who

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:05.340
is maintaining this amazing
database of news articles.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:09.790
If you wanted to read up on it,
just Google the GDELT project.

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:13.180
The GDELT project contains
pretty much every news article.

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:15.100
I might be outstretching
this, but it

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:18.070
has a very big collection
of news articles

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:20.740
from around the world
in lots of languages.

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:24.100
And it has a lot of metadata
about these news articles,

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:27.490
not just the author of
the article or the time

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:30.820
when it was published, the
URL where it was published,

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:34.120
it also has textual
content as well as topics.

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:37.090
And I'm going to show you how
to use the topics from GDELT

00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:39.650
to make predictions.

00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:43.720
So we've got these two
amazing BigQuery datasets,

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:46.150
but I also want to do some
additional processing.

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:48.790
It wasn't enough what I
already had in BigQuery.

00:20:48.790 --> 00:20:50.334
So I used Cloud Dataflow.

00:20:50.334 --> 00:20:52.125
And I'm a product
manager of Cloud Dataflow

00:20:52.125 --> 00:20:55.720
and it's always useful to have
access to unlimited processing

00:20:55.720 --> 00:21:01.180
power to crunch through
all of this data.

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:03.340
So Dataflow was used
for feature processing.

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:05.980
And again, we stored
everything in BigQuery.

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:10.430
All of our features and
labels are stored in BigQuery.

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:10.930
Great.

00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:13.390
So now, we understood
how to build a dataset.

00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:17.710
Let's start having some ideas
about possible predictions.

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:20.070
When I like to do
machine learning,

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:21.910
I like to start with a hunch.

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:25.675
And these hunches tend
to be about user behavior

00:21:25.675 --> 00:21:28.090
or how the business
is being run.

00:21:28.090 --> 00:21:32.950
So my hunch was the contents
of the news article,

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:34.990
as well as where
it was published,

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:36.580
was it the Google's blog post?

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:40.270
Was it maybe a top one site?

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:43.810
Was it kind of a
popular news site?

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:45.880
So these two
predictors are probably

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:48.250
influencing the
outcomes, which are

00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:52.070
who will be reading
this news article

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:55.310
and the communities they
will be sharing them.

00:21:55.310 --> 00:21:55.810
Well, great.

00:21:55.810 --> 00:22:01.030
So out of the four things
on this slide, three of them

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:04.180
are pretty easy to deal
with in machine learning.

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:07.790
The publication domain, the
post submitters and subreddits,

00:22:07.790 --> 00:22:10.480
they're pretty much
categorical variables.

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:12.370
You can convert them
easily into numbers.

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:15.100
And once you have numbers,
you can move them into vectors

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:18.310
and you can train your deep
learning models on them.

00:22:18.310 --> 00:22:23.740
But the first variable
here, the news content,

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:26.310
that's much more
trickier to deal with.

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:28.896
And it is because
it's free form text.

00:22:28.896 --> 00:22:30.520
And although there
are techniques today

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:33.970
to deal with free form
text, they're not perfect.

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:35.470
So these are the
possible techniques

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:37.214
to deal with text and coding.

00:22:37.214 --> 00:22:39.130
So I'm going to talk for
the next five minutes

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:44.410
about how we use some
existing encoding techniques

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:46.840
and brought them together
with deep learning,

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:49.120
and achieved pretty
good results.

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:52.630
Nick talked about
using doc2vec--

00:22:52.630 --> 00:22:56.800
and it's a variant of word2vec
--to make recommendations

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:58.450
for subreddit similarity.

00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:00.610
And it's a great technique.

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:04.150
There's also word2vec
techniques for encoding text

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:06.130
and they have been
used successfully

00:23:06.130 --> 00:23:10.180
for basic sentiment analysis
and topic extraction.

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:12.370
But even before deep
learning came on board,

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:15.470
there was a huge
number and huge volume

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:18.260
of research done on natural
language processing.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:23.830
And I call it pre-deep learning
because word2vec and doc2vec

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:27.730
ended up overshadowing
the previous research done

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:28.940
in that space.

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:31.630
So we actually decided
to use some NLP

00:23:31.630 --> 00:23:36.120
techniques to encode our text
and see how well it worked.

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:38.500
And I'm giving you a
little bit of a preview.

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:41.030
It did work.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:42.610
So here's what we do.

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:44.500
We take a blog
post-- and I'll just

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:48.280
follow the example of
Martin's news article.

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:53.080
So we take this news article and
we extract sentences from text.

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:57.340
And we'll use a Google Cloud
Service, the machine learning

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:00.600
engine, to create syntax trees.

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:04.520
And this is an example of what
is called a dependency tree.

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:07.180
It basically is a
tree of dependencies

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:09.130
between words in a sentence.

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:12.160
There are other variants of
syntax trees, constituency

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:15.850
trees, but pretty much
you can solve the problem

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:19.610
by using what you see
here on the screen.

00:24:19.610 --> 00:24:21.640
Now, once we have
syntax trees, we

00:24:21.640 --> 00:24:24.730
can do a cool thing
called entity recognition.

00:24:24.730 --> 00:24:26.740
I'll give you an example.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:29.020
In my sample sentence,
deep learning

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:31.930
is a popular approach to
building machine learning

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:33.250
models.

00:24:33.250 --> 00:24:35.140
The entity here
is deep learning.

00:24:35.140 --> 00:24:37.750
There's another entity
approach and there is yet

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:41.200
another entity model.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:42.820
In addition to
entity recognition,

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:44.750
you can also do
sentiment analysis.

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:47.980
And nowadays, the
sentiment analysis

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:50.160
can be actually done
on entity level,

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:51.910
not just for the
entire sentence,

00:24:51.910 --> 00:24:53.980
but for specific entities.

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:56.330
So now, we know that deep
learning, for example,

00:24:56.330 --> 00:24:59.200
has a positive score of 0.9.

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:06.300
This indicates a positive
sentiment towards this entity.

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:09.090
Now, there are other
techniques going

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:13.200
beyond simplistic or simple
binary, positive, negative

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:15.180
classification of sentiment.

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:17.490
And these techniques
were developed

00:25:17.490 --> 00:25:20.940
not by computer scientists,
but by psychologists.

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:23.940
In the 1980s, Professor
Plutchik developed

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:26.340
a framework of human emotions.

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:30.690
And he showed that all human
emotions could be classified

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:32.490
into eight basic ones.

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:35.580
For those of you who
can't read the small font,

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:36.910
I'll just list a few of them.

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:41.430
For example, one of the
basic human emotions is joy.

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:44.790
Another one is trust,
yet another is fear.

00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:48.300
Then we have disgust,
sadness, and anticipation.

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:52.020
And then he also showed that
every other human emotion

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:56.610
can be composed as a derivative
of these basic eight.

00:25:56.610 --> 00:26:00.100
For example, love, the
most human emotion of all,

00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:04.660
is actually a combination
of joy and trust.

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:07.590
Now, you can read up and
find some open source

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:10.510
code available on
GitHub by googling

00:26:10.510 --> 00:26:11.910
GitHub [INAUDIBLE] opinions.

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:16.050
I'd say Java library that
classifies text and parses text

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:18.480
according to these emotions.

00:26:18.480 --> 00:26:21.690
So now, we know that we
can do much more with text.

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:24.990
We can divide it into
chunks and these chunks

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:28.230
contain three things, triplets.

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:31.110
What is the person
thinking about an entity?

00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:33.840
What is the opinion of a
person about an entity?

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:38.310
In our sample sentence,
our entity is deep learning

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:42.030
and our opinion is
popular approach.

00:26:42.030 --> 00:26:44.430
Now, you might ask
yourself, who is the person?

00:26:44.430 --> 00:26:48.390
The person is the person who
wrote the entire article.

00:26:48.390 --> 00:26:50.140
So that's how it works.

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:52.230
And once we understood
this, we can

00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:54.930
take pretty much
every textual document

00:26:54.930 --> 00:26:59.160
and convert it into sequences
of entities followed by opinions

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:01.920
and opinions, and then another
entity and another opinion,

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:04.740
and yet another entity and
opinion, et cetera, et cetera,

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:05.240
et cetera.

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:07.740
So it's going to be a long list.

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:09.310
There's no mistake about that.

00:27:09.310 --> 00:27:11.380
But it's a structured list.

00:27:11.380 --> 00:27:14.190
It's a list of just
two types, which

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:15.840
also have some attributes.

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:17.880
So it becomes a
structured problem

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:20.820
instead of having
unstructured text.

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:23.670
And so what we know in
the deep learning space,

00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:26.130
once you have structure
and sequences,

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:29.170
we can go and encode
them into vectors.

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:32.340
And this is how you solve
the encoding problem

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:36.880
because now, you can much
more nicely encode your text.

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:38.380
Another beauty of
this approach is

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:41.620
that it works with pretty much
every topic, existing topic

00:27:41.620 --> 00:27:43.672
classification system.

00:27:43.672 --> 00:27:46.570
GDELT is a great example
of how you can easily

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:53.920
take the topics that GDELT
identified and found in text

00:27:53.920 --> 00:27:56.740
and use it for
encoding text, and then

00:27:56.740 --> 00:28:00.950
using it further
for deep learning.

00:28:00.950 --> 00:28:02.840
If you ask yourself,
what are topics,

00:28:02.840 --> 00:28:05.210
they're basically just
collections of entities.

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:12.315
Here's an example of what GDELT
thinks about Martin's blog

00:28:12.315 --> 00:28:14.330
post.

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:15.660
There are four fields here.

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:17.946
The first one is the
URL of Martin's blog.

00:28:17.946 --> 00:28:18.570
So it's kosher.

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:20.560
It's the real thing.

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:23.130
This is exactly what's contained
in the GDELT repository

00:28:23.130 --> 00:28:25.080
about Martin's blog post.

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:27.420
And then there are
three additional fields.

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:29.630
There's a theme field.

00:28:29.630 --> 00:28:33.960
There's the named entities
field and locations field.

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:36.990
And if you look at
the themes, the things

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:39.000
that GDELT found
in the news article

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:41.850
relate to education,
skills, and jobs.

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:43.830
If you read the article,
you would exactly

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:51.090
match them and concur it is
about skills and learning

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:54.950
TensorFlow, educating yourself.

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:57.210
It is about open source.

00:28:57.210 --> 00:29:04.050
And GDELT was also able to find
some named entities, the Google

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:07.860
Cloud Platform developer,
the machine learning entity,

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:09.210
as well as locations.

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:13.430
Martin's blog contained
references to Belgium.

00:29:13.430 --> 00:29:15.700
So now, we have this
collection of things.

00:29:15.700 --> 00:29:16.690
We have themes.

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:18.160
We have locations.

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:21.320
We have names of
people or things.

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:23.410
And so you throw
this into a bag.

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:25.870
And once you have
a bag, you now know

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:28.000
you can do a bag
of word encodings.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:31.420
And instead of naming or
calling them bag of words,

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:34.090
you're calling them
bags of things.

00:29:34.090 --> 00:29:35.930
And there are various
standard techniques

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:39.580
in deep learning to take
a collection of things

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:44.110
and create a encoding that you
can then further use in machine

00:29:44.110 --> 00:29:46.610
learning and deep learning.

00:29:46.610 --> 00:29:48.800
And this is how they bring
traditional techniques

00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:54.760
for textual analysis with novel
techniques, deep learning.

00:29:54.760 --> 00:29:57.410
We are able to use
categorical variables.

00:29:57.410 --> 00:30:02.240
And this is, by the way,
a chart of a Keras model.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:05.430
And I highly recommend using
Keras if you use TensorFlow.

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:06.950
So this is basically
a flow chart

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:12.590
of how entities and variables
are dependent to each other.

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:14.840
So we take some
categorical variables,

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:18.730
such as the publication domain
or the submitter of the Reddit

00:30:18.730 --> 00:30:24.430
post, and we can integrate it
together with textual content

00:30:24.430 --> 00:30:26.750
through this bag
of things encoding.

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:32.220
And ultimately, we can make
predictions about subreddits.

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:34.660
So our last challenge
