00:39:24.550 --> 00:39:27.910
actually, one of the actual
subreddits this news article

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:30.530
was shared in.

00:39:30.530 --> 00:39:32.870
If you scroll down
in the list, you

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:36.670
will also see the
second subreddit,

00:39:36.670 --> 00:39:39.020
which this news
article was posted on,

00:39:39.020 --> 00:39:41.920
the Artificial subreddit.

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:47.560
Now, you have to
scroll for a long time

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:51.190
until you hit the
third predicted class.

00:39:51.190 --> 00:39:55.840
It is in the list of
predicted classes.

00:39:55.840 --> 00:39:58.990
Doesn't even fit the top 100.

00:39:58.990 --> 00:40:00.280
It does happen.

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:05.200
You can't always
put human behavior

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:07.630
in a mathematical model.

00:40:07.630 --> 00:40:10.300
But having two out
of four, actually

00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:15.110
three out of four among the top
predicted classes is not bad.

00:40:15.110 --> 00:40:21.150
So here's my third subreddit,
which Reddit users actually

00:40:21.150 --> 00:40:23.640
did post this blog post to.

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:27.170
And it's a top 111th prediction.

00:40:27.170 --> 00:40:27.670
Great.

00:40:27.670 --> 00:40:30.980
So now, we have techniques
for multi-label predictions.

00:40:30.980 --> 00:40:33.500
And sometimes, you
get lots of hits.

00:40:33.500 --> 00:40:36.800
Sometimes you get maybe half
of the possible outcomes.

00:40:36.800 --> 00:40:41.310
But having two out of
four is not a bad thing.

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:43.880
So now, let's play
a prediction game.

00:40:43.880 --> 00:40:51.190
In my next scenario, I want to
ask Google or TensorFlow, what

00:40:51.190 --> 00:40:59.520
happened if I discuss just one
additional topic in my model?

00:40:59.520 --> 00:41:06.020
So the blog post today is about
deep learning and TensorFlow.

00:41:06.020 --> 00:41:09.710
What if we discussed just
one additional topic here?

00:41:12.540 --> 00:41:15.750
I'm going to take 2,000 entities
from my list, my dictionary

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:19.770
of entities, and I'm going
to go through each of them

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:24.570
and just compose a new
text of the blog post,

00:41:24.570 --> 00:41:28.163
run it through encoding, and
pick the top 10 predictions.

00:41:31.270 --> 00:41:33.050
And here are the predictions.

00:41:33.050 --> 00:41:35.140
Let's just go through
a couple of them.

00:41:35.140 --> 00:41:41.400
Turns out if Martin,
the author of the blog,

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:43.660
wrote a little bit
about hydroenergy

00:41:43.660 --> 00:41:46.530
and hydroelectricity,
he would have,

00:41:46.530 --> 00:41:51.070
actually, an even higher
chance of being shared

00:41:51.070 --> 00:41:54.020
in the Technology subreddit.

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:57.320
But the top five subreddits
would stay relatively the same.

00:41:57.320 --> 00:41:59.700
It would still be Hacker News.

00:41:59.700 --> 00:42:00.580
It would be Apple.

00:42:00.580 --> 00:42:04.040
It would be Futurology
and the CIDJ--

00:42:04.040 --> 00:42:06.860
I'm actually not even sure
what the subreddit is about.

00:42:06.860 --> 00:42:09.470
Now, let's look at
another prediction.

00:42:09.470 --> 00:42:13.280
What if Martin wrote
about Elon Musk?

00:42:13.280 --> 00:42:18.210
It looks like writing about
Elon almost guarantees you

00:42:18.210 --> 00:42:19.210
good coverage in Reddit.

00:42:22.295 --> 00:42:26.210
The probability of
that new blog post

00:42:26.210 --> 00:42:29.510
that has a mentioning
of Elon Musk

00:42:29.510 --> 00:42:31.730
drives your
predicted probability

00:42:31.730 --> 00:42:35.990
of being shared in
Futurology pretty high.

00:42:35.990 --> 00:42:41.290
And for the other classes,
the predicted subreddits

00:42:41.290 --> 00:42:43.145
stay relatively the same.

00:42:43.145 --> 00:42:44.395
They do vary in probabilities.

00:42:44.395 --> 00:42:46.690
You might get a bit
higher probability

00:42:46.690 --> 00:42:48.200
for some of the classes.

00:42:48.200 --> 00:42:51.370
But if you track what's at
the bottom of this chart,

00:42:51.370 --> 00:42:54.640
you see the same
values again and again.

00:42:54.640 --> 00:42:56.290
OK, so this gives us something.

00:42:56.290 --> 00:42:59.860
Now, we have a tool
to be able to predict

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:03.740
hypothetical scenarios
where we just

00:43:03.740 --> 00:43:07.360
add yet additional topic to
discuss in our blog post,

00:43:07.360 --> 00:43:10.900
and we have immediate
outcomes for possible user

00:43:10.900 --> 00:43:11.850
behavior on Reddit.

00:43:14.650 --> 00:43:17.650
The other scenario that
I wanted to show you was,

00:43:17.650 --> 00:43:22.650
what if we took this blog
post, made no changes

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:27.570
to the contents, but just
published it somewhere else?

00:43:27.570 --> 00:43:30.280
So this code goes
through every publication

00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:34.790
domain known to the model
and it runs predictions.

00:43:34.790 --> 00:43:37.460
And again, it picks the
top 10 predictions based

00:43:37.460 --> 00:43:38.870
on the sum of probabilities.

00:43:38.870 --> 00:43:40.250
That's how they're being ranked.

00:43:44.956 --> 00:43:46.205
OK, so let's see the outcomes.

00:43:50.410 --> 00:43:54.340
It looks like if we
published Martin's blog

00:43:54.340 --> 00:43:57.670
post on aljazeera.com,
we would pretty much

00:43:57.670 --> 00:44:05.830
be guaranteed a share or a post
on the "Al Jazeera" subreddit.

00:44:05.830 --> 00:44:09.310
So the probability
is almost 86%,

00:44:09.310 --> 00:44:14.650
as well as in the [? Villas ?]
7737 news subreddit.

00:44:14.650 --> 00:44:17.140
For those of you who don't
know, "Al Jazeera" actually

00:44:17.140 --> 00:44:20.560
has a news bot that
republishes content that

00:44:20.560 --> 00:44:22.630
was published on aljazeera.com.

00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:24.970
So yeah, it's kind
of an obvious thing.

00:44:24.970 --> 00:44:27.520
If you publish anything
on aljazeera.com,

00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:30.310
it will get
automatically reposted.

00:44:30.310 --> 00:44:34.520
But for some other publication
domains, it's not as obvious.

00:44:34.520 --> 00:44:39.730
For example, if you take
"Scientific American,"

00:44:39.730 --> 00:44:42.520
then you stand a
pretty good chance

00:44:42.520 --> 00:44:49.030
of being republished or reposted
in the Raytheon subreddit.

00:44:49.030 --> 00:44:51.310
What are the other subreddits?

00:44:51.310 --> 00:44:52.720
Well, it's the usual suspects.

00:44:52.720 --> 00:44:56.050
It's technology and
futurology, but they have

00:44:56.050 --> 00:44:57.610
all different probabilities.

00:45:00.280 --> 00:45:03.580
If you have the leverage
of publishing your content

00:45:03.580 --> 00:45:07.420
in different places, now you
can make decisions on, OK,

00:45:07.420 --> 00:45:09.790
I want to go over this
publisher or maybe perhaps

00:45:09.790 --> 00:45:12.260
another publisher.

00:45:12.260 --> 00:45:17.000
Interestingly, I ran through
the test samples before.

00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:22.490
If you ended up getting
published on MIT's "Technology

00:45:22.490 --> 00:45:25.250
Review," you would actually
stand a pretty good chance

00:45:25.250 --> 00:45:27.980
of being republished
in Artificial, which

00:45:27.980 --> 00:45:32.090
was one of the actual subreddits
where this blog post was

00:45:32.090 --> 00:45:33.270
published on.

00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:33.770
Well, great.

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:35.810
So we've seen a
couple of examples

00:45:35.810 --> 00:45:40.250
of how we can use machine
learning to make predictions

00:45:40.250 --> 00:45:42.080
for hypothetical scenarios.

00:45:42.080 --> 00:45:44.180
And for those of you in
the publishing industry

00:45:44.180 --> 00:45:47.420
or people who
write blog posts, I

00:45:47.420 --> 00:45:52.040
hope this was pretty convincing
methodology and tooling

00:45:52.040 --> 00:45:54.795
for making decisions,
what to write about,

00:45:54.795 --> 00:45:55.670
and where to publish.

00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:09.580
To summarize, talking about
accuracy of our models.

00:46:09.580 --> 00:46:10.810
We tried a bunch of things.

00:46:10.810 --> 00:46:15.220
And the things that did work
I talked to you about today,

00:46:15.220 --> 00:46:19.540
but let me summarize
the path to success.

00:46:19.540 --> 00:46:23.350
When we started with just
textual content-- and these

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:24.820
are two models over here.

00:46:24.820 --> 00:46:28.940
When we only use textual
content of news articles,

00:46:28.940 --> 00:46:30.370
we achieve pretty low results.

00:46:30.370 --> 00:46:34.530
Our accuracy was in
the 55, 57% range.

00:46:34.530 --> 00:46:38.380
Only when we added the
publication domain to the mix,

00:46:38.380 --> 00:46:41.063
when we had two variables,
domain and the text,

00:46:41.063 --> 00:46:46.400
did we ended up pushing and
moving towards the 80% range.

00:46:46.400 --> 00:46:48.820
Now, we also tried
out a model where

00:46:48.820 --> 00:46:51.580
we added the
submitter to the mix

00:46:51.580 --> 00:46:53.500
and made it one
of the predictors.

00:46:53.500 --> 00:46:57.550
You cannot use the last model
for predicting future based

00:46:57.550 --> 00:47:04.210
on just the news article,
but you can do pretty amazing

00:47:04.210 --> 00:47:05.930
anomaly detection things.

00:47:05.930 --> 00:47:09.700
So for example, if you observe
user behavior on Reddit

00:47:09.700 --> 00:47:12.910
and it doesn't match this
model, then perhaps something

00:47:12.910 --> 00:47:14.720
interesting is going on.

00:47:14.720 --> 00:47:21.280
Maybe a bot is beginning
posting or maybe a user account

00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:24.270
was taken over by someone else.

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:27.570
We also did some predictions
for popularity score.

00:47:27.570 --> 00:47:29.050
And some of the
earlier predictions

00:47:29.050 --> 00:47:31.210
we've done on models
we built to try

00:47:31.210 --> 00:47:37.300
to do regression and
predict a number, 55,073

00:47:37.300 --> 00:47:39.939
as the score of a news article.

00:47:39.939 --> 00:47:41.980
Those didn't really converge
and we didn't really

00:47:41.980 --> 00:47:44.380
have good outcomes there.

00:47:44.380 --> 00:47:49.780
But once we switched to
classification and bent

00:47:49.780 --> 00:47:53.590
our scores into six
distinct classes,

00:47:53.590 --> 00:47:55.750
we were able to achieve
pretty good results.

00:47:55.750 --> 00:48:00.150
And the confusion matrix
on, I guess, your right,

00:48:00.150 --> 00:48:03.520
shows you a nice
diagonal placement

00:48:03.520 --> 00:48:07.990
of predictions and true labels.

00:48:07.990 --> 00:48:11.500
So I wanted to wrap up and just
summarize what we've seen today

00:48:11.500 --> 00:48:13.820
and what we talked about today.

00:48:13.820 --> 00:48:17.130
We hope that Nick's
and my stories

00:48:17.130 --> 00:48:20.380
were able to convince you that
being able to predict user

00:48:20.380 --> 00:48:24.310
behavior can have significant
impact on the outcome

00:48:24.310 --> 00:48:25.600
of your business.

00:48:25.600 --> 00:48:29.680
Reddit was able to drive
the time on site higher.

00:48:29.680 --> 00:48:32.920
You can use, for example, the
techniques you've seen today

00:48:32.920 --> 00:48:38.230
to better place the content in
different publication domains

00:48:38.230 --> 00:48:42.160
or perhaps choose different
topics you want to write about.

00:48:42.160 --> 00:48:44.830
These techniques are not
unique to social media.

00:48:44.830 --> 00:48:48.460
You can apply them in
pretty much every industry.

00:48:48.460 --> 00:48:51.160
We've seen today how text
encoding and multi-label

00:48:51.160 --> 00:48:54.220
classification
and bags of things

00:48:54.220 --> 00:48:58.660
can help you, even if you are,
perhaps, a car manufacturer

00:48:58.660 --> 00:49:03.250
or IoT device supplier
or a cloud provider,

00:49:03.250 --> 00:49:06.220
to build pretty sophisticated
and powerful machine learning

00:49:06.220 --> 00:49:08.122
models.

00:49:08.122 --> 00:49:09.580
There's a great
collection of tools

00:49:09.580 --> 00:49:13.030
here on the slide, BigQuery,
Cloud Dataflow, Kaggle,

00:49:13.030 --> 00:49:16.360
and Colab,
TensorFlow, obviously.

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:18.490
I also wanted to give
you a couple of links

00:49:18.490 --> 00:49:20.470
for all of the code
that we used today

00:49:20.470 --> 00:49:24.230
for the predictions
and sampling.

00:49:24.230 --> 00:49:26.080
There are kernels on Kaggle.

00:49:26.080 --> 00:49:28.660
There's code on GitHub.

00:49:28.660 --> 00:49:32.860
I want to give a shout out
